{"task_id": "Mbpp/3", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef is_not_prime(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if a given integer is not a prime number.\n    Returns True if the number is not prime (including <= 1 or negative), False if it is prime.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Numbers less than or equal to 1, or negative numbers, are not prime.\n    if number <= 1:\n        return True\n\n    # For natural numbers greater than 1, check for divisors up to its square root.\n    for i in range(2, int(math.sqrt(number)) + 1):\n        if number % i == 0:\n            return True  # Found a divisor, so it's not prime.\n\n    # If no divisors were found, it is a prime number.\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/4", "solution": "```python\nimport heapq\n\ndef heap_queue_largest(numbers: list[int], n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the n largest integers from a given list of numbers, returned in descending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Implementation detail: Use a min-heap to keep track of the n largest elements.\n    # The heap will store the smallest of the n largest elements at its root.\n    if not (1 <= n <= len(numbers)):\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be a positive integer and not exceed the list length.\")\n\n    # Use a min-heap to store the 'n' largest elements found so far.\n    # Python's heapq is a min-heap. To find largest, we can push elements\n    # and maintain heap size 'n'.\n    min_heap = []\n    for num in numbers:\n        if len(min_heap) < n:\n            heapq.heappush(min_heap, num)\n        elif num > min_heap[0]:\n            heapq.heapreplace(min_heap, num)\n\n    # The min_heap now contains the n largest elements. Sort them in descending order.\n    # Sorting a heap takes O(n log n) or we can extract one by one and reverse.\n    # Since we need descending, sorting the list directly is straightforward.\n    result = sorted(min_heap, reverse=True)\n    return result\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/4", "solution": "```python\nimport heapq\n\ndef heap_queue_largest(numbers: list[int], n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the 'n' largest integers from a list and returns them in descending order.\n    Duplicates are preserved if they are among the 'n' largest.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 0 or not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    min_heap = []\n    for num in numbers:\n        if len(min_heap) < n:\n            heapq.heappush(min_heap, num)\n        else:\n            if num > min_heap[0]:\n                heapq.heappop(min_heap)\n                heapq.heappush(min_heap, num)\n\n    # The min_heap now contains the n largest elements.\n    # Convert the heap to a list and sort in descending order.\n    return sorted(min_heap, reverse=True)\n```"}
