{"task_id": "Mbpp/92", "solution": "```python\ndef is_undulating(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given positive integer is undulating.\n    An undulating number's digits strictly alternate between exactly two distinct values.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number <= 0:\n        return False\n\n    s_number = str(number)\n    n_digits = len(s_number)\n\n    if n_digits < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # The first two digits define the alternating pattern.\n    d1 = s_number[0]\n    d2 = s_number[1]\n\n    # If the first two digits are not distinct, it cannot be undulating.\n    if d1 == d2:\n        return False\n\n    # Check if the remaining digits follow the d1, d2 alternating pattern.\n    for i in range(2, n_digits):\n        if i % 2 == 0:  # Even index, should match d1\n            if s_number[i] != d1:\n                return False\n        else:  # Odd index, should match d2\n            if s_number[i] != d2:\n                return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/92", "solution": "```python\ndef is_undulating(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given integer is an undulating number.\n    An undulating number has at least three digits, where digit[i] == digit[i+2] and digit[i] != digit[i+1].\n    \"\"\"\n    digits_str = str(number)\n    digits = [int(d) for d in digits_str]\n    n = len(digits)\n\n    if n < 3:\n        return False\n\n    # Check if digit[i] is equal to digit[i+2] for all valid i\n    for i in range(n - 2): # i goes from 0 to n-3 inclusive\n        if digits[i] != digits[i+2]:\n            return False\n\n    # Check if digit[i] is not equal to digit[i+1] for all valid i\n    for i in range(n - 1): # i goes from 0 to n-2 inclusive\n        if digits[i] == digits[i+1]:\n            return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
