{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "```python\ndef get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total number of sequences of length n,\n    where each element is a positive integer, >= twice the previous, and <= m.\n    \"\"\"\n    # dp_row[val] stores the count of sequences of the current length ending with 'val'.\n    # We use two rows to optimize space: `prev_dp_row` and `current_dp_row`.\n    # Index 0 is unused for convenience; values range from 1 to m.\n    prev_dp_row = [0] * (m + 1)\n\n    # Base case: Sequences of length 1.\n    # For k=1, any single value 'val' from 1 to m forms a valid sequence.\n    for val in range(1, m + 1):\n        prev_dp_row[val] = 1\n\n    # Iterate for sequence lengths from 2 to n.\n    for k in range(2, n + 1):\n        current_dp_row = [0] * (m + 1)\n\n        # Calculate prefix sums for the previous length's DP row (prev_dp_row).\n        # prefix_sums[x] stores sum(prev_dp_row[1] + ... + prev_dp_row[x]).\n        prefix_sums = [0] * (m + 1)\n        for val_idx in range(1, m + 1):\n            prefix_sums[val_idx] = prefix_sums[val_idx - 1] + prev_dp_row[val_idx]\n\n        # Populate current_dp_row for length k.\n        for current_val in range(1, m + 1):\n            # The previous element (prev_val) must be <= current_val // 2.\n            # Sum counts for length k-1 ending with any valid prev_val using prefix sums.\n            max_prev_val_allowed = current_val // 2\n            if max_prev_val_allowed > 0:\n                current_dp_row[current_val] = prefix_sums[max_prev_val_allowed]\n\n        # Move current_dp_row to prev_dp_row for the next iteration.\n        prev_dp_row = current_dp_row\n\n    # The total number of sequences of length n is the sum of all counts\n    # in the last computed DP row (which is now prev_dp_row).\n    total_count = sum(prev_dp_row)\n\n    return total_count\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "```python\ndef get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total number of valid sequences.\n    A sequence of length n has elements x_i such that 1 <= x_i <= m and x_{i+1} >= 2 * x_i.\n    \"\"\"\n    memo = {}\n\n    def _count_recursive(current_val: int, length_remaining: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Recursively counts valid sequences starting with current_val and needing length_remaining elements.\n        Results are memoized to optimize performance.\n        \"\"\"\n        if length_remaining == 0:\n            return 1  # A valid sequence of the desired length has been formed.\n        if (current_val, length_remaining) in memo:\n            return memo[(current_val, length_remaining)]\n\n        count = 0\n        min_next_val = 2 * current_val\n\n        # If the next possible element exceeds m, no valid sequences can extend from current_val.\n        if min_next_val > m:\n            memo[(current_val, length_remaining)] = 0\n            return 0\n\n        for next_val in range(min_next_val, m + 1):\n            count += _count_recursive(next_val, length_remaining - 1)\n\n        memo[(current_val, length_remaining)] = count\n        return count\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0  # Problem states n is a positive integer, so this case implies invalid input.\n    if n == 1:\n        return m  # Any integer from 1 to m is a sequence of length 1.\n\n    total_sequences = 0\n    # The first element x_1 must satisfy 2^(n-1) * x_1 <= m.\n    # Therefore, x_1 <= m / (2^(n-1)).\n    max_start_val = m // (2**(n - 1))\n\n    # Iterate through all possible values for the first element of the sequence.\n    for start_val in range(1, max_start_val + 1):\n        total_sequences += _count_recursive(start_val, n - 1)\n\n    return total_sequences\n```"}
