{"task_id": "Mbpp/448", "solution": "```python\ndef cal_sum(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of Perrin numbers from P(0) to P(n).\n    The Perrin sequence is defined by P(0)=3, P(1)=0, P(2)=2,\n    and P(k) = P(k-2) + P(k-3) for k > 2.\n\n    Args:\n        n: The upper bound index for the sum (inclusive).\n           Must be a non-negative integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        The sum of Perrin numbers from P(0) to P(n).\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input n must be a non-negative integer.\")\n    if n == 0:\n        return 3 # P(0) = 3. Sum of P(0) to P(0) is P(0).\n\n    # Initialize the first three Perrin numbers\n    # p_prev_3 corresponds to P(k-3)\n    # p_prev_2 corresponds to P(k-2)\n    # p_prev_1 corresponds to P(k-1)\n    p_prev_3 = 3  # P(0)\n    p_prev_2 = 0  # P(1)\n    p_prev_1 = 2  # P(2)\n\n    total_sum = p_prev_3 + p_prev_2 + p_prev_1 # Sum of P(0), P(1), P(2)\n\n    # If n is 0, 1, or 2, we return the precalculated sum.\n    # The loop will handle n > 2.\n    if n == 0:\n        return p_prev_3\n    if n == 1:\n        return p_prev_3 + p_prev_2\n    if n == 2:\n        return total_sum\n\n    # Iterate from index 3 up to n (inclusive)\n    for i in range(3, n + 1):\n        current_perrin = p_prev_3 + p_prev_2\n        total_sum += current_perrin\n\n        # Update the previous values for the next iteration\n        # p_prev_3 becomes P(i-2) (which was p_prev_2)\n        # p_prev_2 becomes P(i-1) (which was p_prev_1)\n        # p_prev_1 becomes P(i) (which is current_perrin)\n        p_prev_3, p_prev_2, p_prev_1 = p_prev_2, p_prev_1, current_perrin\n\n    return total_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/462", "solution": "```python\ndef combinations_list(input_list: list) -> list[list]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates all possible unique subsets (combinations) of elements from an input list.\n    Includes the empty list, preserves relative order, and orders combinations as specified.\n    \"\"\"\n    output_combinations = [[]]\n\n    for element in input_list:\n        # Create new combinations by prepending the current element to all existing combinations.\n        new_combinations_this_round = []\n        for existing_combination in output_combinations:\n            new_combination = [element] + existing_combination\n            new_combinations_this_round.append(new_combination)\n\n        output_combinations.extend(new_combinations_this_round)\n\n    return output_combinations\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/462", "solution": "```python\ndef combinations_list(input_list: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates all unique combinations of elements from an input list,\n    adhering to specific ordering requirements.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Initialize the list of combinations with an empty list, representing the combination of no elements.\n    combinations = [[]]\n\n    # Iterate through each element in the input list sequentially.\n    for element in input_list:\n        # Create a snapshot of the current combinations to iterate over,\n        # as new combinations will be added to the main list during this loop.\n        current_combinations_snapshot = combinations[:]\n        # For each existing combination, create a new one by adding the current element.\n        for combo in current_combinations_snapshot:\n            # Prepend the current 'element' to the 'combo'. This specific ordering (new element first)\n            # is critical for matching the desired output sequence and internal element order.\n            new_combo = [element] + combo\n            # Add the newly formed combination to the master list of all combinations.\n            combinations.append(new_combo)\n\n    return combinations\n```\n"}
