{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "```python\n# Helper function 1: Filters positive integers\ndef _filter_positive_integers(arr: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Filters the input array to include only positive integers.\"\"\"\n    return [x for x in arr if x > 0]\n\n# Helper function 2: Finds maximum products of increasing subsequences\n# The design's implementation section describes a dynamic programming approach\n# that directly calculates the maximum product ending at each index for O(N^2) complexity.\ndef _find_increasing_subsequences(arr: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum product of an increasing subsequence ending at each index.\n    This uses a dynamic programming approach to meet performance requirements.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    if n == 0:\n        return []\n\n    # dp[i] stores the maximum product of an increasing subsequence ending with arr[i]\n    # Each element initially forms an increasing subsequence of length 1, with its own value as product.\n    dp = list(arr)\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] * arr[i])\n    return dp\n\n# Helper function 3: Determines the maximum product from a list of products\ndef _determine_max_product(products: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Determines the maximum value from a list of calculated products.\"\"\"\n    if not products:\n        # Returns 1 as the multiplicative identity if no positive increasing subsequence exists.\n        return 1\n    return max(products)\n\ndef max_product(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum product formed by multiplying numbers of an increasing subsequence of that array.\n    This function orchestrates filtering, finding products, and determining the maximum product.\n    \"\"\"\n    filtered_arr = _filter_positive_integers(arr)\n    max_products_ending_at_index = _find_increasing_subsequences(filtered_arr)\n    max_prod = _determine_max_product(max_products_ending_at_index)\n\n    return max_prod\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "```python\ndef tuple_intersection(list1, list2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the intersection of tuples from two lists, ignoring element order within tuples.\n    Normalizes tuples by sorting their elements before performing set intersection.\n    \"\"\"\n    normalized_set1 = set()\n    for tup in list1:\n        normalized_set1.add(tuple(sorted(tup)))\n\n    normalized_set2 = set()\n    for tup in list2:\n        normalized_set2.add(tuple(sorted(tup)))\n\n    result_set = normalized_set1.intersection(normalized_set2)\n    return result_set\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "def max_product(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum product formed by multiplying numbers of an increasing subsequence.\n    Uses a dynamic programming approach to find the maximum product.\n    \"\"\"\n    # BR5: Handle empty array edge case.\n    if not arr:\n        return 1\n\n    # BR6: Handle single-element array edge case.\n    if len(arr) == 1:\n        return arr[0]\n\n    n = len(arr)\n    # dp_products[i] stores the maximum product of an increasing subsequence ending at index i.\n    dp_products = [0] * n\n    # overall_max_product tracks the global maximum product found across all subsequences.\n    overall_max_product = arr[0]\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        current_num = arr[i]\n        # Initialize current_max_product_ending_here with the current number itself (subsequence of length 1).\n        current_max_product_ending_here = current_num\n\n        # Iterate through previous elements to find potential increasing subsequences to extend.\n        for j in range(i):\n            prev_num = arr[j]\n            # BR2: If current_num is strictly greater than prev_num, it can extend the subsequence.\n            if current_num > prev_num:\n                # BR3: Calculate potential product by extending the subsequence ending at j.\n                potential_product = dp_products[j] * current_num\n                # Update current_max_product_ending_here if a larger product is found.\n                current_max_product_ending_here = max(current_max_product_ending_here, potential_product)\n\n        # Store the maximum product ending at current index i.\n        dp_products[i] = current_max_product_ending_here\n        # BR4: Update overall_max_product with the maximum found so far.\n        overall_max_product = max(overall_max_product, dp_products[i])\n\n    return overall_max_product"}
