{"task_id": "Mbpp/589", "solution": "```python\ndef perfect_squares(lower_bound: int, upper_bound: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies and returns a list of all perfect squares within a specified inclusive integer range.\n    Uses an iterative approach, calculating roots to optimize the search.\n    \"\"\"\n    perfect_squares_list = []\n\n    # Perfect squares are non-negative. If upper_bound is negative, no squares exist.\n    if upper_bound < 0:\n        return []\n\n    # Determine the effective lower bound for square root calculation (perfect squares are >= 0)\n    effective_lower_bound_for_sqrt = max(0, lower_bound)\n\n    # Calculate start_root (ceiling of sqrt(effective_lower_bound))\n    # Using float arithmetic and int casting for ceiling (int(x) if x is int, else int(x)+1)\n    # A robust way to implement ceil(x) for non-negative x without math.ceil\n    start_root_float = effective_lower_bound_for_sqrt**0.5\n    start_root = int(start_root_float)\n    if start_root_float > start_root: # Check if there's a fractional part\n        start_root += 1\n\n    # Calculate end_root (floor of sqrt(upper_bound))\n    # Using float arithmetic and int casting for floor (int(x))\n    end_root_float = upper_bound**0.5\n    end_root = int(end_root_float)\n\n    # Iterate from start_root to end_root (inclusive) to find perfect squares\n    for i in range(start_root, end_root + 1):\n        current_square = i * i\n        # Add to list only if within the original specified range\n        if lower_bound <= current_square <= upper_bound:\n            perfect_squares_list.append(current_square)\n\n    return perfect_squares_list\n```"}
