{"task_id": "Mbpp/608", "solution": "```python\ndef bell_Number(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the nth Bell number using dynamic programming.\n    The Bell number represents the number of partitions of a set of n elements.\"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    # Initialize a list to store Bell numbers up to n\n    # bell[i] will store the i-th Bell number\n    bell = [0] * (n + 1)\n    bell[0] = 1\n\n    # Calculate Bell numbers iteratively using the recurrence relation:\n    # B_n = sum_{k=0}^{n-1} (n-1 choose k) * B_k\n    # This method computes Bell numbers directly without explicitly calculating Stirling numbers\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        current_bell_number = 0\n        # Initialize binomial coefficient C(i-1, k)\n        # C(n, k) = C(n, k-1) * (n - k + 1) / k\n        binom_coeff = 1 # C(i-1, 0)\n        for k in range(i):\n            current_bell_number += binom_coeff * bell[k]\n            # Calculate the next binomial coefficient C(i-1, k+1) from C(i-1, k)\n            # C(n, k+1) = C(n, k) * (n - k) / (k + 1)\n            # Here n is (i-1) and k is current k\n            if k < i - 1: # Only calculate if there is a next term\n                 binom_coeff = binom_coeff * (i - 1 - k) // (k + 1)\n        bell[i] = current_bell_number\n\n    return bell[n]\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/619", "solution": "```python\ndef move_num(input_string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Moves all numeric digits in a string to the end, preserving relative order.\n    Non-digit characters maintain their original relative order at the beginning.\n    \"\"\"\n    non_numeric_chars = []\n    numeric_chars = []\n\n    for char in input_string:\n        if char.isdigit():\n            numeric_chars.append(char)\n        else:\n            non_numeric_chars.append(char)\n\n    non_numeric_string = \"\".join(non_numeric_chars)\n    numeric_string = \"\".join(numeric_chars)\n\n    return non_numeric_string + numeric_string\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/620", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_subset(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the size of the largest subset where every pair is mutually divisible.\n    Returns 0 for an empty list.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not nums:\n        return 0\n\n    nums.sort()\n    dp = [1] * len(nums)\n\n    for i in range(len(nums)):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if nums[i] % nums[j] == 0:\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n\n    return max(dp) if dp else 0\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/620", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_subset(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the size of the largest divisible subset from a list of integers.\n    The function uses dynamic programming to find the maximum length.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not nums:\n        return 0\n    if len(nums) == 1:\n        return 1\n\n    nums.sort()\n    dp = [1] * len(nums)\n\n    for i in range(len(nums)):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if nums[i] % nums[j] == 0:\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n\n    return max(dp)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/622", "solution": "```python\ndef get_median(list1: list, list2: list, n: int) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the median of two sorted lists of the same size.\n    It efficiently finds the middle two elements and returns their average.\"\"\"\n    i = 0  # Pointer for list1\n    j = 0  # Pointer for list2\n    count = 0  # Number of elements processed\n    m1 = -1  # To store the (n-1)-th element (0-indexed)\n    m2 = -1  # To store the n-th element (0-indexed)\n\n    # Iterate until we have processed 'n+1' elements from the conceptual merged list.\n    # The (n-1)-th and n-th elements will be stored in m1 and m2 respectively.\n    while count <= n:\n        if i == n:\n            # All elements of list1 have been processed, take from list2\n            m1 = m2\n            m2 = list2[j]\n            j += 1\n        elif j == n:\n            # All elements of list2 have been processed, take from list1\n            m1 = m2\n            m2 = list1[i]\n            i += 1\n        elif list1[i] <= list2[j]:\n            # The current element from list1 is smaller or equal\n            m1 = m2\n            m2 = list1[i]\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            # The current element from list2 is smaller\n            m1 = m2\n            m2 = list2[j]\n            j += 1\n        count += 1\n\n    return (m1 + m2) / 2.0\n```"}
