{"task_id": "Mbpp/739", "solution": "```python\ndef _calculate_triangular_number(k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the k-th triangular number.\"\"\"\n    return k * (k + 1) // 2\n\ndef _count_digits(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Counts the number of digits in a non-negative integer.\"\"\"\n    return len(str(number))\n\ndef find_Index(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the index k of the smallest triangular number that has n digits.\n    The function trusts that n is a positive integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Calculate a lower bound for k to optimize the search.\n    # We need T(k) >= 10^(n-1), so k^2/2 approx >= 10^(n-1), thus k approx >= sqrt(2 * 10^(n-1)).\n    k = max(1, int((2 * (10**(n - 1)))**0.5))\n\n    while True:\n        triangular_num = _calculate_triangular_number(k)\n        digit_count = _count_digits(triangular_num)\n\n        if digit_count == n:\n            return k\n        elif digit_count > n:\n            # If the current triangular number has more digits than required,\n            # it means we skipped the target. This should ideally not happen\n            # with an appropriate starting k, but as a safeguard or if k_start is very aggressive.\n            # In general, if T(k) has > n digits, T(k-1) might have n digits.\n            # However, the problem asks for the *smallest* triangular number with n digits,\n            # so we just need to return the first one we find.\n            # For increasing T(k), once digit_count > n, we've passed the target, so this indicates an issue\n            # or means k started too high. Our k_start logic is safe, so this branch might imply error or\n            # that we're past the number. With incrementing k, this means T(k-1) must have had n-1 digits.\n            # So the condition 'digit_count == n' will be met before 'digit_count > n' when searching upwards.\n            # However, if n=1, k=1, T(1)=1, digit_count=1.\n            # If n=2, k_start=4, T(4)=10, digit_count=2.\n            # The current logic will always find the exact match.\n            pass # Continue to next iteration, k will increment.\n\n        k += 1\n\n```"}
